# TinyDiney API demonstration — IntelliJ HTTP Client
# Select local or railway from http-client.env.json.
# Put guestToken, staffToken and adminToken in http-client.private.env.json
# under the matching environment. Use three different user accounts.
# Tokens must be fresh API access tokens with the scopes in AUTH0_SETUP.md.
#
# Run numbered requests IN ORDER. Response handlers retain generated IDs/ETag.
# This creates one demo restaurant and three bookings; it deletes no records.
# Default to local. Railway mutation requests also persist data.
# See README_TinyDiney.md for setup. Scripts target IntelliJ's HTTP Client.


### 01. Public health — 200
GET {{host}}/health
Accept: application/json

> {%
  client.test("HTTP 200", function () { client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200, got " + response.status); });
  client.test("Application responds", function () { client.assert(response.body.status === "UP"); });
%}

